# Vercel uploads from the REPO ROOT. The project's Root Directory setting
# ("frontend", in .vercel/project.json) only says where to BUILD — not what to
# UPLOAD — so a frontend/.vercelignore is never read and this file has to live
# here. Confirmed from the build log: "Downloading 535 deployment files", which
# is the whole repo, not the 350 under frontend/.
#
# This file also REPLACES .gitignore for upload filtering, which is why the
# ordinary exclusions have to be restated below rather than inherited.

node_modules
dist
build
coverage
.git
.vercel

# Local env files must never reach a build. Being gitignored is NOT enough.
#
# Vite inlines a .env value for any variable that is not ALSO set in the Vercel
# project's own env settings — a real env var wins, an absent one does not. On
# 2026-08-07 (§6.7) that shipped the LOCAL dev VAPID public key to production:
# VITE_API_BASE_URL was safe because Vercel had its own value, but
# VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY did not exist there. Push subscriptions are
# cryptographically bound to the key that created them, so production would
# have handed out subscriptions its own backend could never push to — 403,
# swallowed per-subscription, behind a toggle that looked like it worked.
.env
.env.*

# DO NOT add `backend` here. It was excluded on 2026-08-07 to keep
# backend/.env out of the frontend build, and that was wrong: BOTH projects in
# this repo (smartrays-crm → frontend, smartrays-crm-backend → backend, see
# .vercel/repo.json) upload from the repo root and therefore share THIS file.
# Excluding `backend` would strip the backend project's own source out of its
# own deployment.
#
# The secret it was meant to protect — backend/.env, holding MONGODB_URI,
# JWT_SECRET and the VAPID *private* key — is already covered: the `.env` and
# `.env.*` patterns above have no slash, so they match at every depth,
# including backend/.env and backend/.env.local. The wholesale directory
# exclusion bought nothing except a broken backend deploy.
